<h2 id="waveforms">Waveforms</h2>
<p>Digital signals are signals with clearly defined discrete changes. Analog signals are signals that have smooth changes over time. A digital waveform will look like the picture below. The signal will change state from high (on) to low (off) very quickly. A digital waveform is either on or off, also referred to as high or low. The highs are represented by voltage, usually 5 volts or 3.3 volts DC and the lows are represented by zero volts or ground. A digital high is represented with the number “1” and a low is represented with a “0”.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/electrical-basics/digital-waveform.png"><img src="/assets/electrical-basics/digital-waveform.png" alt="A square wave that switches between a low level and a high level. Labels mark the low and high levels, the rising (leading) edge, and the falling (trailing) edge of a pulse." width="1581" height="632" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A digital waveform. Each pulse rises at its leading edge and falls at its trailing edge.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>In the “real world” waveforms are not perfect squares as shown above. They look more like the waveform shown below. They rise over time (although time may be milliseconds or less) and fall over time. The pulse width is the width of the pulse at its 50% point. The amplitude is the height of the pulse. The rise time and fall time is the amount of time it takes the waveform to rise to full voltage or return to ground. Rise and fall time are measured at 10% and 90% points. This will eliminate error in the measurement caused by interference in the signal, often found at the edges of waveforms.</p>
<figure class="textbook-figure"><a href="/assets/electrical-basics/real-waveform.png"><img src="/assets/electrical-basics/real-waveform.png" alt="A single pulse with rounded corners. Marks at 10%, 50%, and 90% of the height show the rise time and fall time between the 10% and 90% levels, the pulse width between the 50% points, and the amplitude from the low level to the high level." width="1170" height="854" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption><p>A real-world pulse. Rise and fall times are measured between 10% and 90% of the amplitude, and pulse width at the 50% level.</p></figcaption></figure>
<p>Pulse width is the amount of time a pulse is on (high). Frequency is how many times the waveform repeats every second. Period is how long it takes a waveform to complete one full cycle. If you know the period of a pulse you can divide 1/period to find frequency. If you know frequency you can divide 1/frequency to find the period. Pulse width becomes important when we study how servos and electronic speed controllers work as they both use PWM (pulse width modulation), adjusting the width of an electronic pulse, to control either rotation position and direction (servo) or speed (motors).</p>
<p>1/period=frequency</p>
<p>1/frequency=period</p>
<h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
<dl class="key-terms">
<dt>Voltage</dt><dd>Electrical pressure, potential energy.</dd>
<dt>Current</dt><dd>Movement of electrons.</dd>
<dt>Resistance</dt><dd>Opposition to the flow of electrons.</dd>
<dt>Nucleus</dt><dd>Core of an atom. Made of protons and neutrons. Protons have positive charge, neutrons of no charge.</dd>
<dt>Electrons</dt><dd>Orbit the nucleus of an atom. Can be caused to move by applying electrical pressure (voltage).</dd>
<dt>Valence band</dt><dd>Outermost band of an atom.</dd>
<dt>Conductor</dt><dd>An atom with 1 electron in the valence band.</dd>
<dt>Insulator</dt><dd>An atom with 8 electrons in the valence band.</dd>
<dt>Open</dt><dd>No path between power and ground (positive and negative).</dd>
<dt>Short</dt><dd>No resistance between power and ground.</dd>
<dt>Pulse Width</dt><dd>How long an electrical pulse is on (high).</dd>
</dl>
